1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
Last time, we counted chloride by measuring a

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,640
volume of silver nitrate.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,560
This time, we'll weigh it.

4
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,640
A wastewater sample looks perfectly clear.

5
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:12,720
Add barium ions, and a fine white solid

6
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:13,360
appears.

7
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:16,640
After it's filtered and dried, a chemist uses

8
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,800
only its mass to work out how much

9
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:20,160
sulfate was in the water.

10
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,960
So how does a pinch of white powder

11
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:23,840
tell you that?

12
00:00:25,067 --> 00:00:28,666
This is gravimetric analysis, which just means measuring

13
00:00:28,747 --> 00:00:29,466
by mass.

14
00:00:30,027 --> 00:00:31,706
You turn the ion you want into a

15
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:34,346
solid with a known formula, collect all of

16
00:00:34,427 --> 00:00:36,346
it, dry it, and weigh it.

17
00:00:36,907 --> 00:00:39,466
For that mass to mean something, three things

18
00:00:39,547 --> 00:00:40,426
need to be true.

19
00:00:40,987 --> 00:00:42,986
Almost all of the ion has to end

20
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:44,146
up in the solid.

21
00:00:44,427 --> 00:00:46,746
The solid's formula has to be known.

22
00:00:46,987 --> 00:00:48,906
And what you weigh has to be only

23
00:00:48,987 --> 00:00:51,226
that solid, clean and dry.

24
00:00:52,434 --> 00:00:53,833
So which solid?

25
00:00:54,034 --> 00:00:57,073
For sulfate, why barium and not calcium?

26
00:00:57,634 --> 00:01:00,113
Both sulfates have a one to one formula,

27
00:01:00,194 --> 00:01:02,673
so their K s p values compare directly.

28
00:01:03,074 --> 00:01:05,953
Calcium sulfate's is around half a million times

29
00:01:06,034 --> 00:01:08,033
bigger, so a lot of the sulfate would

30
00:01:08,154 --> 00:01:10,033
stay dissolved and never get weighed.

31
00:01:10,434 --> 00:01:13,633
Barium sulfate leaves much less dissolved, and adding

32
00:01:13,714 --> 00:01:16,513
barium in excess pushes that even lower.

33
00:01:16,754 --> 00:01:18,553
That's the common ion effect.

34
00:01:18,834 --> 00:01:21,393
Sodium won't work at all, because sodium sulfate

35
00:01:21,474 --> 00:01:22,273
is soluble.

36
00:01:22,674 --> 00:01:25,713
For chloride, the usual choice is silver, making

37
00:01:25,794 --> 00:01:26,833
silver chloride.

38
00:01:28,034 --> 00:01:29,073
Here's the method.

39
00:01:29,314 --> 00:01:32,753
Dissolve the sample, add dilute hydrochloric acid to

40
00:01:32,834 --> 00:01:37,233
remove carbonate, then add excess barium chloride solution.

41
00:01:37,554 --> 00:01:39,313
A drop more into the clear liquid on

42
00:01:39,474 --> 00:01:42,313
top shows whether all the sulfate is out.

43
00:01:42,674 --> 00:01:46,033
Barium chloride is toxic and silver nitrate stains

44
00:01:46,234 --> 00:01:48,913
skin, so wear gloves and glasses, and the

45
00:01:48,994 --> 00:01:51,113
residues go in heavy-metal waste.

46
00:01:51,394 --> 00:01:53,953
Then digest it: keep it warm and let

47
00:01:53,994 --> 00:01:55,153
it stand.

48
00:01:55,274 --> 00:01:58,433
Small crystals dissolve and bigger ones grow.

49
00:01:58,514 --> 00:02:00,913
Bigger crystals don't slip through the filter paper,

50
00:02:00,954 --> 00:02:02,913
and they trap fewer other ions.

51
00:02:04,134 --> 00:02:06,773
Next, filter it through a filter paper you've

52
00:02:06,854 --> 00:02:08,933
already dried and weighed.

53
00:02:09,014 --> 00:02:10,853
Rinse every bit of solid out of the

54
00:02:10,934 --> 00:02:13,813
beaker with distilled water from a wash bottle.

55
00:02:13,894 --> 00:02:16,533
Then wash the solid on the paper.

56
00:02:16,614 --> 00:02:19,413
Ions like chloride, and the extra barium, are

57
00:02:19,574 --> 00:02:22,213
still in the liquid clinging to the crystals.

58
00:02:22,294 --> 00:02:24,773
If they stay, they dry onto the solid

59
00:02:24,854 --> 00:02:27,573
and add mass that isn't barium sulfate.

60
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:30,880
Now dry the paper and solid in an

61
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:33,520
oven, and cool it in a desiccator, a

62
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:35,920
sealed container with a drying agent, so it

63
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,760
doesn't pick up water from the air while

64
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:38,800
it cools.

65
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:41,200
The balance is zeroed with the empty watch

66
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:43,520
glass on it, so each reading is just

67
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:44,880
the paper and the solid.

68
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:47,160
Then weigh it, and repeat.

69
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:51,520
Heat, cool, weigh, again and again, until two

70
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,240
weighings in a row agree.

71
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:56,400
Then further drying is no longer removing measurable

72
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,800
water: that's constant mass.

73
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:01,920
Washing still matters, though, because dry impurities would

74
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,280
stay on the balance too.

75
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:05,380
Let's use it.

76
00:03:05,860 --> 00:03:08,580
A point five zero zero gram sample gives

77
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:09,580
this data.

78
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:11,620
Find the percentage of sulfate.

79
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:14,980
First, the mass of solid, by difference.

80
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:18,020
The paper and solid, minus the paper alone,

81
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:20,660
gives point three five zero grams of barium

82
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:21,300
sulfate.

83
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,220
Now turn that into moles.

84
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:26,500
Divide by barium sulfate's molar mass, two hundred

85
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:28,740
and thirty three point three nine.

86
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:31,220
That's one point five zero times ten to

87
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:32,980
the minus three moles.

88
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,320
The equation shows one sulfate in every barium

89
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:40,440
sulfate, so that's also the moles of sulfate.

90
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,960
Multiply by sulfate's molar mass, ninety six point

91
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:46,840
zero six, and you get point one four

92
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:48,280
four grams of sulfate.

93
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:51,720
Finally, divide by the sample's mass and times

94
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:52,520
by a hundred.

95
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,960
Twenty eight point eight percent.

96
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:58,200
Careful: it's the sulfate's mass on top, not

97
00:03:58,240 --> 00:03:59,240
the precipitate's.

98
00:04:00,467 --> 00:04:02,146
What if the sample was a volume of

99
00:04:02,307 --> 00:04:03,266
water instead?

100
00:04:03,827 --> 00:04:06,466
Say that same point three five zero grams

101
00:04:06,627 --> 00:04:09,026
came from two hundred and fifty millilitres of

102
00:04:09,067 --> 00:04:09,746
wastewater.

103
00:04:10,307 --> 00:04:12,426
The moles of sulfate don't change.

104
00:04:12,627 --> 00:04:14,866
Divide them by point two five zero zero

105
00:04:15,027 --> 00:04:17,746
litres, and you get six point zero zero

106
00:04:17,987 --> 00:04:20,386
times ten to the minus three moles per

107
00:04:20,467 --> 00:04:20,866
litre.

108
00:04:21,347 --> 00:04:24,146
Times ninety six point zero six, that's about

109
00:04:24,307 --> 00:04:27,026
five hundred and seventy six milligrams per litre.

110
00:04:28,234 --> 00:04:30,713
The balance gives a precise number, but the

111
00:04:30,794 --> 00:04:33,113
method can still push it the wrong way.

112
00:04:33,354 --> 00:04:36,313
Anything that adds mass makes the calculated sulfate

113
00:04:36,394 --> 00:04:39,753
too high, like a damp solid, or impurities

114
00:04:39,834 --> 00:04:41,753
dried on because it wasn't washed.

115
00:04:41,994 --> 00:04:44,873
Anything that loses solid makes it too low,

116
00:04:44,994 --> 00:04:47,433
like crystals through a torn filter paper, or

117
00:04:47,514 --> 00:04:49,153
sulfate left in solution.

118
00:04:49,354 --> 00:04:52,153
And repeating the same faulty method won't fix

119
00:04:52,234 --> 00:04:52,593
it.

120
00:04:52,794 --> 00:04:55,753
Averaging can smooth out random scatter, but an

121
00:04:55,834 --> 00:04:58,513
error that's the same every time stays in

122
00:04:58,554 --> 00:04:59,353
the average.

123
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:02,246
So, two lines to keep.

124
00:05:02,567 --> 00:05:06,726
First, the chain: precipitate mass, to moles, through

125
00:05:06,767 --> 00:05:09,366
the formula ratio, to the ion you want.

126
00:05:09,927 --> 00:05:13,606
Second, the error rule: too wet reads high,

127
00:05:13,687 --> 00:05:15,366
lost reads low.

128
00:05:15,487 --> 00:05:17,766
And always weigh at constant mass.

129
00:05:18,967 --> 00:05:19,886
Your turn.

130
00:05:20,167 --> 00:05:23,206
Creek water is tested for chloride by gravimetric

131
00:05:23,567 --> 00:05:24,206
analysis.

132
00:05:24,487 --> 00:05:27,846
Find the chloride concentration in grams per litre.

133
00:05:28,167 --> 00:05:29,606
Pause here to answer.

134
00:05:40,034 --> 00:05:42,833
Take off the paper first, leaving point one

135
00:05:42,954 --> 00:05:45,193
four three grams of silver chloride.

136
00:05:45,474 --> 00:05:47,633
Divide by one hundred and forty three point

137
00:05:47,714 --> 00:05:49,953
three two for the moles, the same as

138
00:05:49,994 --> 00:05:51,073
the moles of chloride.

139
00:05:51,554 --> 00:05:54,513
Times thirty five point four five, then divide

140
00:05:54,594 --> 00:05:57,953
by point zero five zero zero litres: point

141
00:05:58,034 --> 00:06:00,193
seven zero seven grams per litre.

142
00:06:00,674 --> 00:06:02,993
Forgetting the filter paper makes it nearly seven

143
00:06:03,074 --> 00:06:04,273
times too big.

144
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:06,060
Last one.

145
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:09,580
A student didn't wash a barium sulfate precipitate

146
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:12,860
before drying it, then repeated the whole analysis

147
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:15,620
three times the same way and averaged the

148
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:16,540
results.

149
00:06:16,860 --> 00:06:19,260
Explain the effect on the calculated percentage of

150
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:23,100
sulfate, and assess whether repeating the analysis improves

151
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:23,740
the result.

152
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:25,820
Pause here to answer.

153
00:06:36,234 --> 00:06:39,593
Without washing, soluble ions in the leftover liquid

154
00:06:39,754 --> 00:06:41,113
dry onto the solid.

155
00:06:41,594 --> 00:06:43,673
The mass is too high, so the moles

156
00:06:43,714 --> 00:06:46,713
of barium sulfate, and the sulfate percentage, come

157
00:06:46,794 --> 00:06:47,673
out too high.

158
00:06:48,154 --> 00:06:50,233
Repeating it the same way adds the same

159
00:06:50,394 --> 00:06:51,833
extra mass each time.

160
00:06:52,394 --> 00:06:55,433
Averaging may reduce random scatter, but it can't

161
00:06:55,634 --> 00:06:58,073
cancel an error that's the same every time.

162
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:01,940
So pick a solid that barely dissolves, keep

163
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:04,820
it clean, dry it to constant mass, and

164
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:06,860
the mass tells you the ion.

165
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:10,500
Next: carrying out a gravimetric analysis in the

166
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:11,140
lab.

167
00:07:12,367 --> 00:07:13,806
Here are the key notes.

168
